    # ============ ACADEMIC_WRITING (4) ============
    {
        "context": "academic_writing", "topic_area": "introduction",
        "prompt": "Help me write an introduction paragraph (about 220 words) for my master's thesis on the role of nudge-based interventions in improving retirement savings behavior.",
        "rejected": "## Introduction Paragraph\n\n**The Context:**\nRetirement savings adequacy has become a critical policy issue globally — particularly in countries undergoing demographic transition.\n\n**The Problem:**\n- 🔴 Most workers under-save for retirement\n- 🔴 Traditional financial education has limited impact\n- 🔴 Default options dramatically shape outcomes\n\n**The Behavioral Economics Lens:**\n- 💡 People are not perfectly rational\n- 💡 Defaults, framing, timing all matter\n- 💡 Small 'nudges' can have outsized effects\n\n**What This Study Examines:**\n- Field experiments on nudge interventions\n- Across three retirement savings contexts\n- Measuring impact on enrollment, contribution rates, and persistence\n\n**Why It Matters:**\n- 💰 Direct policy implications\n- 💰 Low-cost interventions if they work\n- 💰 Generalizable principles for behavior change",
        "chosen": "The adequacy of retirement savings has emerged as a significant policy concern across many economies, particularly those undergoing rapid demographic transitions. Despite decades of financial education programs and tax-advantaged savings vehicles, a substantial proportion of workers continue to enter retirement with savings well below recommended levels, a pattern that imposes growing costs on individuals, families, and public welfare systems. Traditional explanations of this shortfall, rooted in assumptions of rational economic behaviour, have proven inadequate to account for the persistence of under-saving even among well-informed and high-income individuals. The development of behavioural economics over the past three decades has offered an alternative perspective, drawing attention to the role of cognitive biases, present bias, and the powerful influence of choice architecture on financial decisions. Within this tradition, nudge-based interventions, which seek to alter behaviour through subtle changes to the way choices are presented rather than through coercion or financial incentives, have been the subject of growing scholarly and policy attention. The present thesis investigates the role of nudge-based interventions in improving retirement savings behaviour, drawing on a series of field experiments conducted across three distinct retirement savings contexts. By examining the impact of these interventions on enrolment, contribution rates, and the persistence of savings behaviour over time, the study aims to contribute both empirical evidence and practical guidance for the design of more effective retirement savings policies."
    },
